@suchenzang i obv care about good writing, and obv this isnt applicable to high sensitivity domains like literature, but the interesting thing about the aaronson mechanism is that the bias only applies to close-enough tournament candidates. if we trust that llms model language well, then this should genuinely kinda work and scale better the more that llms also route usecases well
[->] @suchenzang as you know there's a curve so just depends on the cutoff you choose on low-entropy but claude gave me these numbers. top token p of 0.5 seems decent. 

i think point just stands that either we trust that the llm can model language enough to accurately calibrate token distribution or not, and i've done some basic reading but have yet to find any convincing argument on the actual harm of the watermarking here given these condition. and if the llm doesnt model {literature|writing|law|whatever} well then... yknow... bitter lesson it??
